# Chapter 7 · The History and Evolution of Women's Quotas in Pakistan

> "**Elaborate the historical context and evolution of women's quota in Pakistan** concerning key legislation and policies that have shaped the quota system" (==2025==). The 2021 question's "**17 percent quota**" refers to 60 of the 342 National Assembly seats created in 2002. MCQs asked for **60** seats (2024), **33%** under devolution (2016), the paper's **17%** (2016) and the **two** women of the first Constituent Assembly (2016, 2024).

## 7.1  The colonial roots

Reserved seats for women in the subcontinent began under British rule. The **Government of India Act** (==1935==) reserved seats for women in the federal and provincial legislatures and widened the women's franchise. Women such as **Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz** — who had attended the **Round Table Conferences** (1930–32) in London — won seats in the Punjab Assembly in ==1937==. Reserved seats thus came to Pakistan as part of its colonial inheritance.

## 7.2  The timeline

::: fig timeline | The evolution of women's quotas in Pakistan.
@layout vertical
1935: Government of India Act — reserved seats for women in legislatures
1947: First Constituent Assembly — 2 women of 69 members
1956: Constitution — 10 seats (5 East, 5 West Pakistan); women could also contest general seats
1962: Constitution — 6 seats (3 + 3), filled by indirect election
1973: Constitution — 10 NA seats for 10 years or three elections, plus provincial seats
1985: Raised to 20 NA seats under General Zia
1988: Last election with reserved seats; they then lapsed
1990–97: Three elections with no reserved seats; very few women elected
2000–01: Devolution of Power Plan — 33% of local council seats
2002: LFO — 60 NA seats, 17 Senate seats, 128 provincial seats; NA enlarged to 342
2008: Women's Parliamentary Caucus formed
2017: Elections Act — 5% tickets for women; 10% turnout rule
2018: FATA merger — KP's reserved seats rise; NA reduced to 336 (60 women's seats kept)
:::

## 7.3  Phase 1 — Early constitutions (1947–1971)

- The **first Constituent Assembly** (1947) had just **two** women among 69 members: **Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz** and **Begum Shaista Suhrawardy Ikramullah**. They pressed for the **Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act** to give women inheritance in agricultural land and for women's rights in the constitution.
- The **1956 Constitution** reserved **10 seats** for women in the unicameral National Assembly — **five each** from East and West Pakistan — for ten years. Women could also contest general seats.
- The **1962 Constitution** of Ayub Khan reserved **six seats** (three from each wing), filled by **indirect election** through the Basic Democrats and assemblies.

Reserved seats in this phase were few and indirectly filled, and the members were mostly elite women from political families and the **All Pakistan Women's Association (APWA)**.

## 7.4  Phase 2 — The 1973 Constitution and Zia (1973–1988)

The **1973 Constitution** reserved **10** National Assembly seats for women (and a small number of provincial seats), for **ten years or until the third general election**, whichever came later. Members were elected **indirectly** by the assemblies.

Under **General Zia-ul-Haq**, the reserved seats were increased to **20** for the ==1985== non-party election. Paradoxically, this came under a regime whose **Hudood Ordinances** (==1979==) and **Law of Evidence** (==1984==) harmed women's rights — and Zia's Majlis-e-Shoora included women appointees. Women members of this period were few and mostly unable to challenge these laws.

## 7.5  Phase 3 — The lapse (1988–2002)

The constitutional provision expired after the ==1988== election, the third under the Constitution. In the elections of ==1990==, ==1993== and ==1997== there were **no reserved seats**, and only a handful of women — **between two and six** — won general seats. Ironically, this was the decade of **Benazir Bhutto**'s two governments; she promised to restore the seats but neither her governments nor Nawaz Sharif's did so, partly because they lacked the two-thirds majority or the political will.

::: simple
The lapse is the clearest evidence for quotas in Pakistan: when the quota disappeared, women almost disappeared from parliament. With two to six women in a house of over 200, the National Assembly was over **97%** male.
:::

## 7.6  Phase 4 — Devolution and the 2002 quota

### (1) Local government — 33% (2000–01)

General **Pervez Musharraf**'s **Devolution of Power Plan** (==2000==) and the **Local Government Ordinance** (==2001==) reserved **33%** of seats for women in union, tehsil and district councils (MCQ 2016). Around **36,000** women were elected as councillors in ==2000–01== — the largest entry of women into politics in Pakistan's history. Many seats in conservative areas went unfilled, and some women were proxies for male relatives, but many gained experience and confidence.

### (2) National and provincial — 60 seats (2002)

The **Legal Framework Order (LFO) 2002**, later validated by the **17th Amendment** (==2003==), enlarged the National Assembly to **342** and reserved **60** seats for women — about **17.5%** (the "17 percent quota" of the 2021 question and the 2016 MCQ). It reserved **17** seats in the Senate and **128** in the provincial assemblies. The seats are filled by **proportional representation** from party lists, in proportion to the general seats each party wins.

The **National Commission on the Status of Women** (==2000==) and the **National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women** (==2002==) — which called for women's representation in decision-making — formed part of the same policy package. The impact was immediate: women's share of the National Assembly rose from under **3%** to over **20%** in ==2002==.

## 7.6a  How reserved seats are allocated — a worked example

The women's seats in the National Assembly are divided among the **provinces** (after the 2023 census delimitation: Punjab 32, Sindh 14, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa 10, Balochistan 4). Within each province, parties receive women's seats **in proportion to the general seats they win** there, under **Article 51(6)(d)**; independents who join a party within three days of the results are counted with that party.

::: simple
Suppose a party wins **half** of Punjab's general seats. It receives about **half** of Punjab's 32 women's seats — **16** — and fills them from the top of the priority list it submitted before the election. A party that wins no general seats gets no reserved seats, however many votes it received.
:::

This system explains two features of Pakistani politics: reserved-seat women are **loyal to party leaders**, who decide list order, and the seats become a prize in **disputes over party membership** — as in the ==2024–25== dispute over PTI-backed independents.

## 7.7  Phase 5 — Consolidation and new reforms (2008 onwards)

- The **18th Amendment** (==2010==) retained the reserved seats and Article 34.
- The **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** (==2008==) turned numbers into laws (Chapter 5).
- Provincial **local government laws** (from ==2013==) kept women's seats, but most cut the share below the 2001 level of 33%.
- The **Elections Act 2017** introduced the first **candidate quota** (**5%** of general-seat tickets, Section 206), the **10% women's turnout** rule (Section 9), and special measures for registration where the gender gap exceeds 10%.
- The **25th Amendment** (==2018==) merged the tribal areas into Khyber Pakhtunkhwa; the National Assembly was reduced to **336** seats, but the **60** women's seats were kept, and KP's reserved provincial seats rose.

## 7.7a  Reform proposals in the debate

Women legislators, the NCSW and civil-society groups have put forward several proposals for the next stage of the quota's evolution:

1. **Raise the share** of reserved seats towards **33%**, in line with the Beijing target and the 2001 local-government quota.
2. **Direct election** of women's seats — through rotating reserved constituencies or dual-member constituencies — so that women have voters and a power base of their own.
3. **Strengthen Section 206** of the Elections Act 2017: raise the candidate quota from 5% and require that women be fielded in **winnable** seats, with sanctions for non-compliance.
4. **Restore and protect local quotas** of at least 33% in every province's local-government law.
5. **Transparent party lists**, chosen through internal party elections rather than by leaders alone.

These proposals respond directly to the weaknesses of the present system — indirect election, party dependence and the "ceiling" effect — and give you a ready-made **way forward** for questions on quotas (2016, 2021, 2024, 2025).

## 7.8  Assessing the evolution

::: fig bars | Women in the National Assembly under different quota regimes (approximate number of women members).
@colour 2F7D4F
1985 (20 reserved): 22
1997 (no quota): 6
2002 (60 reserved): 74 *
2018 (60 reserved): 69
:::

::: balance | Has the quota system evolved in the right direction?
- **Gains:** a permanent, larger quota since 2002; a candidate quota since 2017; legal protection of women's votes; women's representation at about one-fifth; laws for women passed by women.
- **Gaps:** seats still filled **indirectly** by party leaders; the 5% ticket rule is weak; few women win general seats (11 in 2024); local quotas reduced; the quota has become a **ceiling** in party minds.
-> **Line to take:** Pakistan's quota has moved from **symbolic** (1956–88) to **substantial** (since 2002), but not yet to **empowering**. The next step is **direct election** and stronger candidate quotas.
:::

::: eye | The model structure for 2025
1. Introduction: quota defined; Pakistan's mix of reserved seats and candidate quota. 2. Colonial roots (1935). 3. Early constitutions (1956: 10; 1962: 6). 4. 1973 and Zia (10 → 20). 5. The lapse (1988–2002) and its lesson. 6. Devolution (33%) and the LFO (60, 17, 128). 7. 2008–2017 (caucus, 18th Amendment, Elections Act: 5% and 10%). 8. Assessment. 9. Reforms. 10. Conclusion. Draw the timeline.
:::
